Lab 17: Configuring Networking for Virtual Machines
Objectives
By the end of this lab, students will be able to:

Configure and manage logical networks in Red Hat Virtualization Manager (RHVM)
Create and configure MAC address pools for virtual machine network interfaces
Test and verify VM network connectivity across multiple virtualization hosts
Understand the fundamentals of virtualized networking in enterprise environments
Troubleshoot common networking issues in virtualized infrastructures
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, students should have:

Basic understanding of Linux networking concepts (IP addresses, subnets, VLANs)
Familiarity with Red Hat Enterprise Linux command line interface
Knowledge of virtualization concepts and terminology
Understanding of network interface configuration in Linux
Basic experience with Red Hat Virtualization Manager interface
Required Knowledge Areas
TCP/IP networking fundamentals
Linux system administration basics
Virtual machine concepts
Network troubleshooting tools (ping, traceroute, netstat)
Lab Environment Setup
Ready-to-Use Cloud Machines: Al Nafi provides pre-configured Linux-based cloud machines for this lab. Simply click Start Lab to access your environment - no need to build your own virtual machines or install software.

Your lab environment includes:

Red Hat Virtualization Manager (RHVM) server
Two virtualization hosts (hypervisors)
Pre-installed virtual machines for testing
Network configuration tools and utilities
Task 1: Set Up a Logical Network in RHVM
Subtask 1.1: Access RHVM Administration Portal
Connect to RHVM Web Interface

Open your web browser and navigate to the RHVM administration portal:

https://rhvm-server.lab.local/ovirt-engine
Login with Administrator Credentials

Use the provided credentials:

Username: admin@internal
Password: RedHat123!
Navigate to Network Configuration

Click on Compute in the left navigation panel
Select Networks from the dropdown menu
You will see the default ovirtmgmt network already configured
Subtask 1.2: Create a New Logical Network
Create New Network

Click the New button to create a new logical network.

Configure Network Properties

Fill in the following details:

Name: lab-network-17
Description: Lab 17 Virtual Machine Network
VLAN Tagging: Check this option
VLAN ID: 100
VM Network: Check this option (enables VM connectivity)
MTU: Leave as default (1500)
Advanced Network Settings

Click on the Cluster tab:

Select your cluster (usually Default)
Check Required to make this network mandatory
Check Display Network if you want to use this for display traffic
Save Network Configuration

Click OK to create the logical network.

Subtask 1.3: Attach Network to Hosts
Navigate to Hosts

Click on Compute → Hosts
Select your first virtualization host
Configure Host Network Interface

Click on Network Interfaces tab
Click Setup Host Networks
Drag the lab-network-17 network to an available network interface
Configure the network interface:
# Example network configuration
Interface: eth1
Network: lab-network-17
Boot Protocol: Static IP
IP Address: 192.168.100.10
Subnet Mask: 255.255.255.0
Apply Configuration

Click OK to apply the network configuration to the host.

Repeat for Additional Hosts

Repeat the same process for your second virtualization host:

IP Address: 192.168.100.11
Same subnet mask and network settings
Subtask 1.4: Verify Network Configuration
Check Network Status

In the RHVM interface, verify that:

The logical network shows as Up status
All hosts show the network as In Sync
No error messages appear in the events log
Command Line Verification

SSH to each host and verify network configuration:

# Connect to first host
ssh root@host1.lab.local

# Check network interfaces
ip addr show

# Verify VLAN interface
ip addr show eth1.100

# Check routing table
ip route show
Task 2: Configure MAC Address Pools for VM Network Interfaces
Subtask 2.1: Create Custom MAC Address Pool
Access MAC Address Pool Configuration

In RHVM, click on Administration → Configure
Select MAC Address Pools from the left panel
Create New MAC Pool

Click New to create a custom MAC address pool:

Name: lab-mac-pool-17
Description: Custom MAC pool for Lab 17 VMs
Allow Duplicates: Unchecked
Define MAC Address Range

Add MAC address ranges for your pool:

From: 00:1a:4a:17:00:00
To: 00:1a:4a:17:ff:ff
Click Add to include this range in the pool.

Save MAC Pool Configuration

Click OK to create the MAC address pool.

Subtask 2.2: Assign MAC Pool to Data Center
Navigate to Data Centers

Click on Compute → Data Centers
Select your data center (usually Default)
Edit Data Center Properties

Click Edit
In the General tab, find MAC Address Pool
Select lab-mac-pool-17 from the dropdown
Click OK to save changes
Subtask 2.3: Configure VM Network Interfaces with Custom MAC
Create Test Virtual Machine

Navigate to Compute → Virtual Machines
Click New to create a new VM:
Name: test-vm-network-17
Operating System: Red Hat Enterprise Linux 8.x x64
Memory: 2048 MB
CPU: 2 cores
Disk: 20 GB
Configure Network Interface

In the VM creation dialog:

Click on System tab
Under Network Interfaces, click Add
Configure the interface:
Name: nic1
Profile: lab-network-17
Type: VirtIO
MAC Address: (will be auto-assigned from pool)
Verify MAC Assignment

After creating the VM, check that it received a MAC address from your custom pool:

Select the VM
Click on Network Interfaces tab
Verify the MAC address starts with 00:1a:4a:17
Subtask 2.4: Create Additional Test VMs
Clone Test VM

Create two more VMs for network testing:

# VM Names:
test-vm-network-17-host1
test-vm-network-17-host2
Distribute VMs Across Hosts

Assign test-vm-network-17-host1 to run on Host 1
Assign test-vm-network-17-host2 to run on Host 2
